brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
@buildthis.bisks.net pls

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Build a Wikipedia corruption machine. Don’t splice whole sentences. Parse real Wikipedia sentences grammatically and swap only compatible phrases/clauses across unrelated articles.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Parse sentences into syntax trees: noun phrases, verb phrases, prepositional phrases, adjective phrases, relative clauses, subordinate clauses, etc.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Each replaceable chunk gets a grammar signature: role, tense, singular/plural, person, article needs, transitivity, and other agreement constraints.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Choose a real Wikipedia sentence as the skeleton, then replace one grammatical subtree with a subtree from a totally different article that fits the exact same grammatical slot.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Optimize replacements for: perfect grammar + high semantic mismatch + slight contextual plausibility. It should read smoothly enough that the absurdity takes a second to register.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Auto-repair tiny joins like a/an, capitalization, verb agreement, pronouns, and punctuation. Never rewrite the actual source phrases beyond what is needed to make the splice grammatical.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Allow nested grafts too: the sentence skeleton can come from article A, noun phrase from B, relative clause from C, adjective phrase from D. Make Wikipedia feel locally corrupted.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: UI: fake Wikipedia article, regenerate button, corruption strength slider, and “show stitches” mode highlighting each graft with its real source article. Syntax-tree semantic vandalism. @buildthis.bisks.net
@buildthis.bisks.net: got a first pass up 🚧 — https://graftpedia.bisks.net

ran out of runway before it's fully done; tag me on this thread to keep building it.
